// br_delay -- the Pyrope equivalent of verilog/br_delay.sv.
//
// Hand-written rather than emitted from the Verilog, so that the synthesis
// comparison measures the two LANGUAGES and not the two front ends. The shape
// is idiomatic Pyrope: the upstream `genvar` loop over `BR_REGI` macros becomes
// an unrolled `for` over an array register, and the packed 2-D `out_stages`
// port becomes explicit per-range bit assigns (Pyrope has no `{a,b}` form).
//
// EVERYTHING IS A LITERAL, deliberately. This lhd build rejects an array size
// that is a named constant, let alone an expression over one ("a named
// constant must fold before lowering"), and it has removed the
// `int(min=,max=)` type in favour of sized `uN`. So the module is monomorphic
// at Width = 8, NumStages = 4 -- and design.toml pins exactly that one config.
// The numbers below are therefore load-bearing:
//
//   4  = NumStages           u8  = Width
//   40 = (NumStages + 1) * Width, the flattened out_stages bus

// `in` is Pyrope's membership operator, so the port name -- which must match
// the Verilog for LEC to line the two sides up -- is backtick-escaped.
//
// clk and rst are DECLARED even though Pyrope would supply both implicitly: the
// interface has to match verilog/br_delay.sv port for port, or lec pairs a
// 3-port module against a 5-port one.
//
// `rst` is muxed into the register DATA rather than wired to a reset pin, and
// that is deliberate. `reg stages:[4]u8 = 0` on an ARRAY is a reset value, but
// an array is a memory and a memory has no parallel reset port -- Pyrope
// realizes it as a one-entry-per-cycle restore SWEEP, so a full reset takes
// FOUR cycles. Upstream's `BR_REGI(stages[i], stages[i-1], InitValue)` is a
// flop with a one-edge synchronous reset, so a single-cycle `rst` pulse makes
// the two designs genuinely differ (lgcheck's bounded miter leaves `rst` free
// and finds it). The explicit `if rst != 0 { stages[j] = 0 }` arm below is the
// one-edge parallel reset the SystemVerilog actually describes.
pub mod br_delay(clk:u1, rst:u1, `in`:u8) -> (out:u8@[0], out_stages:u40@[0]) {
  // The 4 pipeline registers, all reset to InitValue (0 upstream).
  reg stages:[4]u8:[clock_pin=ref clk] = nil  // reset is the `rst` arm below, not a pin

  out = stages[3]

  // The flattened stage bus. Lane 0 is the input and lane i+1 is stage i,
  // which is exactly the indexing upstream documents: out_stages[0] == in and
  // out_stages[NumStages] == out.
  mut packed:u40 = 0
  packed#[0..=7] = `in`
  for i in 0..<4 {
    packed#[((i + 1) * 8)..=(((i + 1) * 8) + 7)] = stages[i]
  }
  out_stages = packed

  // The shift itself, written last because a register's write is a cycle-
  // boundary effect: every read ABOVE this point sees this cycle's q, exactly
  // as the Verilog's non-blocking assignments do.
  //
  // Written HIGH STAGE FIRST. A Pyrope reg array defaults to
  // `ordering="program"` -- reads and writes resolve in program order, like
  // software -- so an ASCENDING loop would read the `stages[i-1]` this same
  // loop just wrote, and `in` would fall through all four stages in ONE cycle
  // (LEC: out_stages 0xfefefefe00 against the Verilog's 0xfe00). Descending,
  // every read of `stages[j-1]` still precedes that entry's own write, which
  // is exactly the non-blocking semantics upstream's `BR_REGI` has.
  // `:[ordering="old"]` on the declaration says the same thing directly and
  // works too; the loop order is kept here because it needs no attribute.
  for i in 0..<4 {
    const j = 3 - i
    if rst != 0 {
      stages[j] = 0
    } elif j == 0 {
      stages[0] = `in`
    } else {
      stages[j] = stages[j - 1]
    }
  }
}
